'''Ngardi''', also spelt '''Ngarti''' or '''Ngardilj''', is an [[Australian Aboriginal languages|Australian Aboriginal language]] that is considered [[moribund language|moribund]]. It was previously thought to be an alternative name for the [[Bunara language]], but these are now classified as separate languages. It was/is spoken by the [[Ngardi]] people of the [[Northern Territory]] and northern [[Western Australia]].

==Classification==
Capell (1962) considered Ngardi, [[Warlpiri language|Warlpiri]], and [[Warlmanpa language|Warlmanpa]] to be dialects of a single language. [[R. M. W. Dixon]] (2002) grouped Ngardi together with Warlpiri and Warlmanpa in the [[Ngarrkic languages|Yapa group]], but admitted that this was based on limited data. McConvell and Laughren (2004) showed that it was in [[Ngumbin languages|Ngumbin]], a closely related group, and this was followed in Honeyman (2005). However, Bowern (2011) listed it as a more distant [[Wati languages|Wati language]].<ref>Bowern, Claire. 2011 [http://anggarrgoon.wordpress.com/2011/12/23/how-many-languages-were-spoken-in-australia/ "How Many Languages Were Spoken in Australia?"], ''Anggarrgoon: Australian languages on the web'', 23 December 2011 ([http://pamanyungan.sites.yale.edu/master-list-australian-languages-v12 corrected] 6 February 2012)</ref>

Tindale shows Ngardi as an alternative name for Bunara language, but [[Lynette Oates]] and [[Arthur Capell]] showed that Bunara was a separate language. The two languages have now been assigned separate code in [[AIATSIS]]'s [[AUSTLANG]] database.<ref>{{AIATSIS|A121|Ngardi}}</ref><ref>{{AIATSIS|A69|Bunara}}</ref>

==Waringari==
Some old recordings and manuscripts refer to '''Waringari''' (or '''Waiangara''') as a language related to Ngardi, but linguists have agreed that it is a geographical name and not the name of a language.<ref>{{AIATSIS|A63|Waringari}}</ref> [[Norman Tindale]] listed Waringari as a pejorative name for the Ngarti people,{{sfn|Tindale|1974|p=234}} as well as for the [[Yeidji]],{{sfn|Tindale|1974|p=243}} the [[Worla]]{{sfn|Tindale|1974|p=255}} and the [[Warlpiri people|Warlpiri]], suggesting that they were [[Human cannibalism|cannibal]]s.{{sfn|Tindale|1974|p=237}}

